WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.110
If you drive down a certain stretch of highway
in the California desert, you will hear music.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.350
It's supposed to sound a little bit like this:

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:13.460
[William Tell Overture, by Rossini]

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.480
but instead, it sounds like this.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:31.340
[William Tell Overture, by Rossini,
but badly out of tune]

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.180
That was the musical road
of Lancaster, California.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.680
And as you may have heard,
it's a little bit out of tune.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.520
A lot of money was spent on that road.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.070
It was a publicity stunt for a car company
who wanted to show off how clever they are.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.170
And doing the calculations for
something like that isn't really difficult.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.460
A musical note is a vibration in the air at
a particular frequency.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.989
And those vibrations can be made by
a violin string or synthesised by a computer

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:01.120
or - in this case - made by vehicle tyres
hitting grooves in the road.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.209
The closer the grooves are,
the faster the vibrations, and the higher the note.

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:06.579
So take the speed limit,

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:09.069
divide by the frequency of the note you want,

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.829
and that's how often there should be a groove
in the road.

00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:14.929
And if people travel at the wrong speed, well,
it shouldn't really matter.

00:01:14.929 --> 00:01:18.770
It'll just be like slowing down or speeding up
any other piece of music:

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.560
it'll be in the wrong key, but it'll still
sound right to most people's ears.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.279
It won't sound like that mess.

00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:27.979
So what went wrong?

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:30.479
At this point, I am indebted to David Simmons-Duffin,

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.259
an assistant professor of physics at Caltech,

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.280
whose interests include Quantum Field Theories
and playing baroque violin.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.139
Because he was, as far as I can tell,

00:01:39.139 --> 00:01:42.929
the very first person to figure out
what went wrong:

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:46.270
and the problem was probably the English language.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.799
Whoever did the calculations said that the
grooves should be so far apart.

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:52.170
Let's say, four inches for the first note.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.450
And they meant there should be
a groove every four inches.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.169
But whoever gave the instructions
to the work crew

00:01:58.169 --> 00:02:00.800
said that the grooves should be
"four inches apart".

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.180
And that was interpreted as four inches between
the end of one groove

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.079
and the start of the next one.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:10.759
They didn't include
the width of the groove itself.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:14.140
But your ears definitely do include it.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.540
The highest note on that section of
the William Tell Overture

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.750
should be one octave higher than the lowest.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.780
That means the frequency should be doubled,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.739
and that the grooves should be
half the distance apart.

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:29.290
And they are: if you don't include
the width of the groove itself.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.909
That error means that every note is distorted
not by a fraction of its value,

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:34.599
like if you're travelling at the wrong speed,

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:37.930
but by a constant amount: by the width of
the grooves.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.560
The higher the note, the greater the effect
of that distortion.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.910
And the result is the mess that you are about
to hear for a second time.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.629
And the really strange part?

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:51.469
This is the second musical road
built in Lancaster, California.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.370
The first one, built by the car company, was
too close to residents,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.140
who complained about the constant noise.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.420
And probably this note here.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:03.600
So the city paved over it and rebuilt it...

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.420
to the exact, same, wrong blueprints.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.700
That's a take.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.880
That is a take.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:14.890
Right there.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.200
And in order to celebrate that take, I am
going to put my foot down.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.139
This is an SUV.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.180
It doesn't, er... it doesn't do that.

